    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 261128\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n428 AM MST Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Temperatures will remain within the normal range through\n  Thursday with lower desert highs mostly in the eighties.\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather conditions will last through the middle\n  of week with increasing clouds starting Wednesday.\n\n- An unsettled weather pattern will return late week and last\n  through the weekend with the best rain chances on Friday and\n  Sunday. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nQuiet weather conditions have settled into the region under\nquasi-zonal westerly flow aloft. A dry air mass now in place will\nkeep skies clear to mostly clear through Tuesday, while\ntemperatures rise a bit under the modestly higher heights aloft. \nDaytime highs are expected to reach into the mid 80s across the \nlower deserts starting today and last through the middle part of \nthe week despite increasing clouds starting Wednesday. Overnight \nlows will also fall within the normal range through Wednesday with\nlower desert readings mostly between 55-60 degrees.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe weather pattern is expected to shift late this week with a\ndeep trough getting carved out over the Pacific Northwest and\nsouthwestward to off the coast of California by Thursday. The\npolar jet associated with the northern stream system is expected\nto dip farther southward off the West Coast, while a stronger\nsubtropical jet across the central Pacific shifts eastward toward\nBaja. Although these jet branches are not expected to phase\ntogether going into Friday, there is likely to be some combined \nenergy reaching the Western U.S. \n\nAs the trough sets up to our west on Wednesday into Thursday, high\nlevel moisture will stream into our region, followed by increasing\nsoutherly moist flow in the lower levels Thursday night into\nFriday. Guidance is in very good agreement southerly low level \nmoist advection will combine with some top-down saturation in the \nupper levels on Friday across southern and central Arizona. PWATs \nare shown to quickly rise to just above 200% of normal by Friday \nmorning, while low level mixing ratios rise to a respectable 8-9 \ng/kg. This decent moisture is also expected to coincide with \nmodest low level upslope forcing and quite strong mid and upper \nlevel ascent within the left exit region of the subtropical jet. \nAlthough we are still four days out, confidence is quite good in \nshower activity blossoming at some point Thursday night/Friday \nmorning across south-central Arizona and lasting through much of \nFriday. Guidance also shows modest MUCAPE of 250-500 J/kg on \nFriday which should allow for some isolated to scattered weak \nthunderstorms. Shear values look to be on the low side, so at this\npoint we are not anticipating any strong storms. Forecast QPF \nfrom Thursday night through Friday night currently averages around\n0.50\" across the Phoenix area to upwards of 0.75-1.00\" in the \nhigher terrain north of Phoenix. The higher end of guidance is at \nleast double that, so it's not out of the question to have to \nworry about some minor flooding issues Friday. WPC has also \nincluded this area in a Slight Risk on their Day 5 ERO.\n\nThe Friday weather event will not be the last period of rain\nexpected over the next week as another likely even stronger system\nis forecast to track east northeastward toward southern California\nand northern Baja on Saturday before moving through our region\non Sunday. There is much more uncertainty with this second \nweather system compared to the first as there are notable timing \nand evolution differences. There are also some differences with \nthe potential phasing of the polar and subtropical jet streams \nwhich if it occurs would likely result in a decent low pressure \nsystem developing within the base of the large scale trough. \nModels at least agree that there will be as much if not better \nmoisture then the Friday system and stronger mid-level and frontal\nascent. Given the uncertainty, QPF amounts are still quite \nvolatile to any big changes in the strength of the system and \ntrack. However, so far ensemble mean QPF amounts indicate overall \nhigher average amounts compared to Friday's event, as well as more\nwidespread coverage. Rain chances could also extend into Monday, \nespecially across eastern portions of Arizona.\n\nTemperatures will take a hit later this week and through the\nweekend with NBM forecast highs dropping into a 75-80 degree range\nFriday and Saturday and even lower starting Sunday. The coolest \nair so far this fall should move in with the Sunday weather \nsystem as highs may fall to as the upper 60s to lower 70s for \nSunday and Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1127Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation weather concerns expected through the TAF period\nunder few high cirrus. The overall wind pattern will continue to\nfollow typical diurnal tendencies, but with wind speeds mostly\nunder 6 kts. There will also be extended periods of light\nvariability to calm conditions. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns expected through the TAF period under\nmostly clear skies. Wind speeds will mostly stay below 6 kts with\na westerly component favored at KIPL and predominantly variable at\nKBLH. Extended periods of light variability to calm conditions can\nbe expected at KIPL as well. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWeak high pressure will remain over the region early this week\nallowing for gradual drying and temperatures within the normal \nrange. Minimum humidity levels will continue to fall, reaching \n20-35% today and 15-30% Tuesday. Overnight recoveries will remain\ngood at 40-65%. Winds will remain light each day favoring typical\ndiurnal patterns. A shift in the weather pattern late this week \nand into next weekend should bring about cooler, wetter, and \nbreezier conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Benedict/Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
